<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tharu people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tharu are an indigenous people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> lowlands along the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Long resistant to malaria, they farmed the forested plains when few others lived there, and they speak Indo-Aryan Tharu languages. From the 1950s malaria eradication and migration from the hills cost them much land, and many were bonded labourers until the kamaiya system was abolished in 2000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tharu-people.jpg" alt="Tharu people" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Punya · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tharu_Mahila.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tharu are recognised as an official ethnicity by the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and as a scheduled tribe by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a>. The origin of the name is uncertain. It may come from sthavir, a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, and Tharu in the central Nepali Terai see themselves as the original people of the land and descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Gautama Buddha</a>. The Rana Tharu of western Nepal connect the name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a> and claim descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> who moved to the forests in the 16th century, a claim some scholars reject. According to Alberuni, Tharu people had lived in the eastern Terai since at least the 10th century. In 2011 Nepal counted 1,737,470 Tharu, 6.6% of its population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> in the late 18th century, members of the ruling families received land grants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> and the right to collect revenue from the cultivators. The Tharu became bonded labourers in a system called Kamaiya. In 1854 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jang-bahadur-rana">Jung Bahadur Rana</a> enforced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muluki-ain">Muluki Ain</a>, a general code that classed the Tharu among the touchable, enslavable, alcohol-drinking groups. In the late 1950s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-health-organization">World Health Organization</a> helped the Nepalese government eradicate malaria in the forests of the central Terai, and after the programme with DDT in the 1960s many people from the hills and elsewhere settled there. Many Tharu families in the western Terai lost their land and were forced to work as Kamaiya.</p><p>When Chitwan's first protected areas were set up, Tharu communities were made to leave their lands, and when Chitwan National Park was designated, soldiers destroyed villages inside it. They were denied any right to own land, and so were left landless and poor.</p><p>In July 2000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> abolished bonded labour under the Kamaiya system and declared the loan papers illegal. The families were freed from their debts but were left homeless and jobless. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepalese-civil-war">Nepalese Civil War</a> the Tharu endured intense violence, and after the peace accord of 2006 Tharu organisations called for an autonomous Tharu state within a federal Nepal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Tharu consider themselves a people of the forest. In Chitwan they have lived in the forests for hundreds of years, practising short-fallow shifting cultivation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, wheat, mustard, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and lentils and gathering forest products. They show resistance to malaria that research has linked to genes for thalassemia, although the immunity is limited, not complete, and many Tharu, especially babies, died of the disease. Different groups speak different languages, including variants of Bhojpuri in the centre and Maithili in the east. Their art includes Mokha wall painting, Ashtimki painting and Sikki grass crafts, and festivals include Maghe Sankranti and Jur Sital. Rana Tharu traditionally live in longhouses called Badaghar, with families of up to 31 members under a Mukhiya.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tharu people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Makwanpur Sens ruled hill and Terai territory with a mixed population of Magars, <b>Tharus</b> and Hindu settlers, drawing revenue from agriculture and trade with Bihar.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-makwanpur">Kingdom of Makwanpur</a></li></ul></section>
